1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:09,812
Welcome back! Last time, we built a ray tracer. One ray for every pixel, bent by the black hole, running on the graphics card.

2
00:00:10,333 --> 00:00:18,115
But a real black hole is almost never alone. Gas spirals in toward it, and on the way down, it heats up until it glows.

3
00:00:18,633 --> 00:00:29,564
Today we add that disk. And by the end of this video, you'll have the full picture from the start of the series, and you'll know exactly where the halo and that lopsided glow come from.

4
00:00:31,367 --> 00:00:42,554
Our disk is thin and flat. It sits in one plane, and stretches from six M, the innermost stable orbit, out to twenty M. Here it is from the side, with the camera just above that plane.

5
00:00:43,034 --> 00:00:47,106
One ray goes straight to the near side of the disk. Nothing weird there.

6
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:54,700
But a ray aimed above the hole gets bent down, onto the far side. So we see the back of the disk, lifted up over the shadow.

7
00:00:55,167 --> 00:01:00,221
And a ray aimed below the hole gets bent up, and hits the far side from underneath.

8
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:09,540
So we should see three images of one flat disk. That's the picture we're about to build. And here's my favourite part: we don't write any special code for the halo. It just shows up.

9
00:01:11,733 --> 00:01:17,684
The disk is thin and flat, so finding it is easy. Here's the loop from last time, with the new lines added.

10
00:01:18,166 --> 00:01:21,007
Before each step, we remember where the ray was.

11
00:01:21,766 --> 00:01:30,309
After the step, we compare the two heights. The disk lies in the plane z equals zero. So if z changed sign, the ray must have gone through that plane.

12
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:37,902
Where exactly? t is the fraction of the step where the height hit zero, and mix slides between the two positions by that fraction.

13
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:48,133
Disk light gives back a colour, and how opaque the gas is at that spot. We add the colour, and shrink 'remaining': the share of light that can still come from further away.

14
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:57,105
And then the ray just keeps going. It might reach the stars. Or it might swing around and cross the disk again. That's where the extra images come from. No extra code.

15
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:03,537
Now let's make it spin. The gas is in orbit, and you can work out how fast with school physics.

16
00:02:04,033 --> 00:02:11,641
To move in a circle, something has to supply the centripetal force: m v squared over r. Here, gravity does that job.

17
00:02:12,133 --> 00:02:17,824
Cancel the small mass, solve for v, and the orbital speed is the square root of G M over r.

18
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:29,489
Divide by r and you get the angular speed, omega: how fast the gas goes around. Gas close in goes around way faster than gas further out. Watch the inner dots overtake the outer ones.

19
00:02:29,967 --> 00:02:36,338
And here's something lovely: Einstein's theory gives exactly the same formula for circular orbits around a black hole.

20
00:02:37,933 --> 00:02:44,480
Into the shader. If the crossing point is inside six M, or beyond twenty M, there's no disk there, so we return nothing.

21
00:02:44,967 --> 00:02:50,028
Otherwise, omega is the formula we just found. Remember, our M already has the G built in.

22
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:57,594
And the velocity is omega times the position, turned through a right angle. Swap x and y, and flip one sign.

23
00:02:58,067 --> 00:03:02,949
At the inner edge of the disk, that works out to roughly half the speed of light. Half!

24
00:03:04,033 --> 00:03:13,132
To see the motion, the shader needs to know what time it is. So every frame, the program reads the clock and sends it down as one more uniform.

25
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:22,993
The shader paints streaks on the disk with a noise pattern. Each point's angle gets shifted by omega times the time. So every ring turns at its own orbital speed.

26
00:03:24,567 --> 00:03:34,636
Now the colour. You know the Doppler effect from an ambulance driving past. The siren sounds higher coming toward you, and lower going away. Light does exactly the same thing.

27
00:03:35,134 --> 00:03:40,441
Gas coming toward us looks bluer and brighter. Gas moving away looks redder and dimmer.

28
00:03:40,934 --> 00:03:48,882
And there's a second effect. Light climbing away from the hole loses energy, like a ball thrown upward. But light can't slow down. So it turns redder instead.

29
00:03:49,367 --> 00:03:58,639
We can pack both effects into a single number, g: the energy of the light when it reaches us, divided by its energy when it left the gas.

30
00:04:00,234 --> 00:04:09,162
Here it is in the shader. First, two clock factors. Near a black hole, clocks run slow: a lot for the fast-moving gas, and a little for our camera.

31
00:04:09,634 --> 00:04:19,755
Emitted is the light's energy, as the gas measures it. See the dot product of p and v? That's how much the light's motion lines up with the motion of the gas. That's your Doppler effect, right there.

32
00:04:20,234 --> 00:04:28,392
Why the minus sign? Good question. Our ray runs from the camera to the gas. The real light went the other way. So its momentum is flipped.

33
00:04:28,867 --> 00:04:34,389
Received is the energy as our camera measures it. And g is one divided by the other.

34
00:04:35,467 --> 00:04:45,587
Now, hot things glow. Think of a stove element going from dull red, to orange, to white. The gas is hottest near the inner edge, and cooler further out.

35
00:04:46,067 --> 00:04:58,056
What we see is that temperature multiplied by g. Glow colour turns it into a colour. And the brightness is scaled by g to the fourth power. So a small change in g means a big change in brightness.

36
00:04:59,134 --> 00:05:04,530
Here's the disk with g switched off. Evenly lit, all the way around. Kind of boring.

37
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:14,012
And here it is with g. The side spinning toward us blazes, and the other side fades. You'll find that lopsided glow in every real image of a black hole.

38
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:22,698
One problem left. The bright side of the disk is dozens of times brighter than the dim side, and way brighter than the stars.

39
00:05:23,167 --> 00:05:32,352
But a normal image stores each colour as a number between zero and one. Anything brighter gets cut off, and turns into flat, boring white.

40
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:46,390
So we stop drawing straight to the screen. First we create a texture: an image that lives on the graphics card. The format, R G B A sixteen F, means each colour is a floating-point number, so values way above one survive.

41
00:05:46,867 --> 00:05:55,966
Then a framebuffer. Normally OpenGL draws into the window. A framebuffer lets us point it somewhere else. Here we attach our texture, so the drawing lands in that image.

42
00:05:57,033 --> 00:06:06,727
Each frame now has two passes. Pass one: bind our framebuffer, and trace the picture exactly like before. It lands in the texture, with all its brightness intact.

43
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:16,597
Pass two: bind framebuffer zero, which means the window. Then draw the big triangle again, with a second, tiny shader that reads the texture.

44
00:06:17,567 --> 00:06:21,606
That shader looks up this pixel in the texture, and runs it through a curve.

45
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:31,753
If we just chopped everything off at one, all the bright parts would go flat. The curve squeezes large values smoothly toward one instead, so you can still see detail inside the glare.

46
00:06:32,233 --> 00:06:37,113
The last line corrects for how monitors display brightness, and writes the final colour.

47
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:41,743
And there it is. Stage three, running.

48
00:06:42,267 --> 00:06:50,257
The halo over the top is the far side of the disk, its light bent toward us. The thin arc underneath is the far side again, seen from below.

49
00:06:50,767 --> 00:06:57,268
The left side blazes because that gas is rushing toward us. And the whole disk turns, each ring at its own speed.

50
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:05,366
And remember: nothing here was drawn by hand. Two rules for light, a flat disk, and one number for the colour. That's it.

51
00:07:05,867 --> 00:07:19,687
Look how far we've come. Stage one: the two rules, traced on the processor and drawn as lines. Stage two: the same rules in a shader, one ray per pixel. Stage three: a disk, its motion, and its glow.

52
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:29,389
The repository also has a more polished version of this program. It adds a soft glow around the bright areas, and a camera that properly accounts for curved space.

53
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:41,511
And it has tests. They check the code against things you can work out by hand: that light orbits at three M, that the shadow's edge is at five point two M, and that distant rays bend by the textbook amount.

54
00:07:42,033 --> 00:07:49,982
And that's a whole black hole, built from scratch. If you made it this far, go run the code, break it, and make it yours. Thanks for watching!

